            <ArticleTitle>Second-look arthroscopy with removal of bioabsorbable tacks.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>125-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Eleven years after tearing her anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) (not reconstructed), a 36-year-old dancer reinjured her knee and required arthroscopic ACL reconstruction. At arthroscopy, the medial meniscus had a bucket-handle tear that was repaired by using three bioabsorbable tacks. The ACL was then repaired in the usual manner. Because of persistent posterior knee pain throughout her rehabilitation, we performed &quot;second-look&quot; arthroscopy 14 weeks after reconstruction. The meniscus had healed and was stable; however, tack motion was evident and the tacks were easily removed. Inspection of the tacks showed that the barbs had been resorbed. The patient recovered uneventfully, and pain-free flexion 28 days after surgery was 0 degrees-136 degrees. We believe this to be the first reported case demonstrating the early stages of tack degradation in meniscal repair.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
